{#-
  How the README says what the benchmarks found: one macro per kind of block.

  Python measures and stores facts -- rows, settings, the host, what was skipped -- and
  these macros are the only place those facts become sentences. Each macro takes the
  block dict that `suite.blocks()` builds and returns Markdown with no trailing newline,
  so the template around it decides the spacing.

  Every paragraph of output is one line, as the README has always had them; that is why
  some lines here are long.
-#}

{% macro thousands(value) %}{{ "{:,}".format(value) }}{% endmacro %}

{#- A sentence fragment, empty unless the dataset scores a sample of its users. -#}
{% macro sampled(b) %}{% if b.dataset.max_eval_users is not none %} Scored on a fixed random sample of {{ thousands(b.dataset.max_eval_users) }} held-out users.{% endif %}{% endmacro %}

{#- A sentence fragment, empty unless the dataset leaves models out. -#}
{% macro left_out(b) %}{% if b.skip %} Not run: {% for name, reason in b.skip.items() %}{{ name }} ({{ reason }}){{ "; " if not loop.last else "" }}{% endfor %}.{% endif %}{% endmacro %}

{% macro facts(h) %}{{ h.cpu }} ({% if h.cores %}{{ h.cores }} usable cores{% else %}unknown core count{% endif %}), {{ h.platform }}, {{ h.python }}, numpy {{ h.numpy }}, skrecsys {{ h.skrecsys }}, `_core` built in {{ h.build }} mode{% endmacro %}

{% macro device(h) %}{% if h.get("device") %} The sequential models ran with `device="auto"`, which resolved to {{ h.device.device }} on this host (torch {{ h.device.torch }}); every other model is CPU only.{% endif %}{% endmacro %}

{#-
  Where the rows were timed. One run names one host, as the README always has; rows
  re-measured separately can come from different machines, and then the block says
  which ran where, since a timing only compares against one taken on the same host.
-#}
{% macro hosts(b) -%}
{%- if b.hosts | length == 1 -%}
Measured on {{ facts(b.hosts[0].facts) }}. The quality table above is deterministic and portable; the timings below are not comparable across machines or builds.{{ device(b.hosts[0].facts) }}
{%- else -%}
Measured on more than one host, so compare timings only between rows that share one. {% for group in b.hosts %}{{ group.models | join(", ") }}: {{ facts(group.facts) }}.{{ device(group.facts) }} {% endfor %}The quality table above is deterministic and portable; the timings below are not comparable across machines or builds.
{%- endif -%}
{%- endmacro %}

{% macro timing(s) %}Wall clock per call. Each operation is sampled until it has spent {{ "{:.0f}".format(s.budget) }}s or reached its cap ({{ s.repeat }} fits, {{ s.rank_repeat }} `recommend` calls), after untimed warm-up calls ({{ s.warmup.fit }} fit, {{ s.warmup.rank }} rank) so that no sample pays for a cold start; the `samples` columns say how many each row actually got, which is why a slow model shows fewer. The batch columns say what a single call processed: one fit covers the whole training split, and one `recommend` call ranks the entire catalog for every held-out user at once, so these are throughput numbers rather than single-request latency. Fit reports the spread a handful of samples can resolve; ranking is sampled often enough for nearest-rank quantiles, each of which is a call that really happened. Compare `min` across machines and watch `max` for the variance a run saw.{% endmacro %}

{#- A paragraph each for what has no result and what has an outdated one; empty when neither. -#}
{% macro rows_notes(b) %}{% if b.missing %}

Not yet measured: {{ b.missing | join(", ") }}.{% endif %}{% if b.stale %}

Measured under an earlier version of its config entry, and due a re-run: {{ b.stale | join(", ") }}.{% endif %}{% endmacro %}

{% macro index_list(groups, total) %}{% for group in groups %}{{ group.index }} for {% if group.models | length == total %}every model{% else %}{{ group.models | join(", ") }}{% endif %}{{ "; " if not loop.last else "" }}{% endfor %}{% endmacro %}

{% macro index_notes(b) %}{% if b.missing %}

Not yet measured: {{ index_list(b.missing, b.total_models) }}.{% endif %}{% if b.stale %}

Measured under an earlier version of the index, and due a re-run: {{ index_list(b.stale, b.total_models) }}.{% endif %}{% endmacro %}

